insert into blog_posts (title, slug, excerpt, content, cover_image, category, published, published_at) values

('Coping Emotionally With a Cancer Diagnosis: A Supportive Approach',
'coping-emotionally-with-cancer-diagnosis-supportive-approach',
'A cancer diagnosis brings immense emotional weight for patients and families alike. Here is how emotional and homeopathic support can work alongside oncology treatment to ease this journey.',
'<h2>The Emotional Weight of a Diagnosis</h2><p>A cancer diagnosis affects far more than the body — it brings fear, uncertainty, grief, and disruption to daily life for both patients and their families. Emotional wellbeing during this time is just as important as physical treatment.</p><h2>Reason: Why Emotional Support Is Often Overlooked</h2><p>Cancer care understandably focuses heavily on medical treatment — surgery, chemotherapy, radiation — under the guidance of an oncologist. In this focus, the emotional toll of anxiety, sleeplessness, fear, and caregiver exhaustion can be under-addressed, even though it significantly affects a patient''s quality of life and resilience through treatment.</p><h2>Solution: A Complementary, Whole-Person Approach</h2><p>Homeopathy, used as a <strong>complementary support alongside — never a replacement for — prescribed oncology treatment</strong>, can help address some of the emotional and quality-of-life challenges that accompany a cancer journey, such as:</p><ul><li>Anxiety and fear surrounding diagnosis, treatment, or scans</li><li>Disturbed sleep during and after treatment</li><li>General emotional exhaustion and low mood</li><li>Supporting overall wellbeing alongside medical care</li></ul><p>It is essential that any complementary care is discussed openly with your treating oncologist, so it works in coordination with — and never interferes with — your primary cancer treatment plan.</p><h2>Supporting a Loved One Through Cancer</h2><ul><li>Listen without trying to "fix" every fear they express</li><li>Help maintain small routines that offer a sense of normalcy</li><li>Encourage them to stay connected with their full care team, including emotional and psychological support</li><li>Take care of your own wellbeing too, as caregiver burnout is real and common</li></ul><h2>A Message of Support</h2><p>Facing cancer is one of life''s most difficult journeys. Combining strong oncological care with emotional and complementary support can help patients and families navigate it with greater resilience and comfort.</p>',
'https://picsum.photos/seed/cancer-emotional-support/900/600',
'common-issues', true, now()),

('Understanding Oncology Treatment: What Patients and Families Should Know',
'understanding-oncology-treatment-what-patients-families-should-know',
'Navigating an oncology treatment plan can feel overwhelming. Here is a clear overview of what to expect — and how holistic, emotional support fits alongside medical care.',
'<h2>What Oncology Care Typically Involves</h2><p>Oncology treatment is highly individualised, depending on cancer type, stage, and overall health. It commonly includes some combination of surgery, chemotherapy, radiation therapy, or targeted treatments, all guided by a specialist oncology team.</p><h2>Reason: Why the Journey Feels Overwhelming</h2><ul><li>Multiple appointments, tests, and specialists to coordinate</li><li>Physical side effects of treatment affecting energy and daily routine</li><li>Uncertainty about outcomes, creating ongoing anxiety</li><li>Balancing treatment with work, family, and financial concerns</li></ul><h2>Solution: Building a Well-Rounded Support System</h2><p>Alongside your primary oncology treatment — which should always remain the central, guiding plan from your specialist — a well-rounded support system can ease the journey considerably:</p><ul><li><strong>Medical team:</strong> your oncologist and specialists guiding the core treatment plan</li><li><strong>Emotional support:</strong> counselling or therapy to process fear, grief, or anxiety</li><li><strong>Complementary care:</strong> individualised homeopathic support for general wellbeing, sleep, and emotional resilience, always coordinated with your oncologist</li><li><strong>Community support:</strong> family, friends, or patient support groups who understand the journey</li></ul><h2>Questions Worth Asking Your Care Team</h2><ul><li>What does the recommended treatment plan involve, step by step?</li><li>What side effects should we prepare for, and how can they be managed?</li><li>Is it safe to combine any complementary or supportive care with the current treatment?</li><li>What resources exist for emotional and caregiver support?</li></ul><h2>A Reminder for Patients and Families</h2><p>You do not have to navigate this alone. A strong care team — medical, emotional, and complementary — working together in coordination gives patients the best possible support through every stage of treatment.</p>',
'https://picsum.photos/seed/oncology-treatment-guide/900/600',
'trending', true, now())

on conflict (slug) do nothing;
